{
  "id": "COMPOUND:33837 - PROTEIN:3897",
  "verbalization": "Calanphenanthrene A is implicated in the regulation of cholesteryl ester transfer protein (CETP) through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that Calanphenanthrene A acts as a target for multiple compounds, including (+)-Parviflorol, 1-O-beta-D-Glucopyranosyl anthranilate, and 4-Ketopinoresinol, all of which are known to interact with the Serine/threonine-protein kinase pim-3. This kinase, in turn, is a target of these compounds and is ultimately linked to CETP through a cascade of molecular relationships. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism by which Calanphenanthrene A may influence CETP activity, possibly through the modulation of pim-3, which could alter lipid metabolism and thus affect CETP function. The evidence supports a plausible, though not yet fully elucidated, link between Calanphenanthrene A and CETP, mediated through the pim-3 kinase pathway.",
